## 11. Walkthrough: llama.cpp text generation under vlinux

`ROADMAP_LINUX_RUNTIME.md` U8: a real static-glibc `llama-completion`
(llama.cpp's text-generation CLI) generating real text under `--runtime
linux` on the fast leg -- verified working end to end (`-n 32`) against
Bonsai-4B-Q1_0.gguf, prompted with "The capital of France is":

> The capital of France is Paris. Paris is the second-largest city in
> France, with a population of over 1.8 million. Paris is located in the
> Île-de-France

(Bonsai-4B-Q1_0 is an extreme 1-bit quantization -- "second-largest" is a
model-quality artifact, not an andesim/vlinux correctness issue; the
point here is that a real 4B-parameter model's real GGUF loading,
tokenization, RVV matrix math, and sampling all produced coherent,
on-topic English text end to end.) This needs the vendor static-glibc
`LINUX_TOOLCHAIN` (`config.env`/env; see section 5.13) and a checkout of
[llama.cpp](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp).

### Build

A fresh **static** build, separate from any dynamic build you may already
have (a dynamic build cannot run here -- there is no dynamic linker under
vlinux):

```sh
TC=$LINUX_TOOLCHAIN   # the static-glibc toolchain, config.env's LINUX_TOOLCHAIN
cmake -B build-rv64-static \
  -DCMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=Linux -DCMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR=riscv64 \
  -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=$TC/bin/riscv64-linux-gcc \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=$TC/bin/riscv64-linux-g++ \
  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
  -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS=-mext-zvlsseg -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS=-mext-zvlsseg \
  -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS=-static \
  -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF \
  -DGGML_RV_ZFH=OFF -DGGML_RV_ZVFH=OFF -DGGML_CPU_REPACK=OFF \
  -DGGML_OPENMP=OFF -DGGML_NATIVE=OFF \
  -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_COMMON=ON -DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=ON

cmake --build build-rv64-static -j$(nproc) --target llama-completion
```

What each non-obvious flag is for:
- `-static` (the linker flag, not just `BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF`): without
  it the binary still dynamically links libc/libstdc++/the dynamic
  linker itself -- there is no dynamic linker under vlinux, so this is
  required, not optional.
- `-mext-zvlsseg`: the Andes toolchain gates RVV segment-load/store
  intrinsics behind this flag; ggml's RVV kernels use them.
- `GGML_RV_ZFH=OFF GGML_RV_ZVFH=OFF`: the target engine (`ax45mpv_premium`,
  ELEN=32) has no scalar or vector half-precision float support; leaving
  these on produces code that traps.
- `GGML_CPU_REPACK=OFF`: repacking picks tensor layouts tuned for
  specific microarchitectures vlinux is not modeling.
- `GGML_OPENMP=OFF`: no OpenMP runtime here. This does NOT remove
  threading -- ggml's CPU backend still spawns its own thread pool via
  `pthread_create` when `-t` asks for more than one thread. vlinux has no
  `clone`/`clone3` support (no threads, ROADMAP_LINUX_RUNTIME.md's own
  non-goal), so **always pass `-t 1`** at run time.
- `LLAMA_CURL=OFF`: no network stack here; only needed for llama.cpp's
  optional model-download-by-URL feature.

**One required source patch**, independent of `-t 1`: llama.cpp's own
async status logger (`common/log.cpp`) spawns a background `std::thread`
unconditionally, on the FIRST log call, regardless of `-t` -- and the
generated completion text itself is printed through this same logger
(`LOG("%s", token_str.c_str())`), not a separate direct-stdout path, so
it cannot simply be avoided or ignored. Since `clone` is `ENOSYS` here,
that `std::thread` construction throws `std::system_error`, uncaught,
crashing the process before `main()`'s own code ever runs. This is a
genuine upstream robustness gap (a background-logging performance
optimization should not be able to crash the whole program if thread
creation fails, on ANY platform), not a vlinux-specific issue -- fixed by
catching the construction failure in `common_log::resume()` and falling
back to printing synchronously from `add()` instead of queueing for a
worker that will never run. Apply this patch to your llama.cpp checkout
before building:

```diff
--- a/common/log.cpp
+++ b/common/log.cpp
@@ struct common_log {
     bool prefix;
     bool timestamps;
     bool running;
+    bool synchronous = false; // true if the worker thread could not be
+                              // created (e.g. no thread support on this
+                              // platform) -- add() prints inline instead
@@ void add(...) {
         ...
         entry.timestamp = 0;
         if (timestamps) {
             entry.timestamp = t_us() - t_start;
         }

+        if (synchronous) {
+            // No worker thread exists (see resume()) -- print immediately
+            // rather than queueing for a consumer that will never run.
+            print_entry(entry);
+            return;
+        }
+
         tail = (tail + 1) % queue.size();
         cv_new.notify_one();
     }

     void resume() {
         ...
         running = true;

-        thrd = std::thread([this]() {
-            ...
-        });
+        try {
+            thrd = std::thread([this]() {
+                ...
+            });
+        } catch (const std::system_error &) {
+            synchronous = true;
+        }
     }

     void pause() {
         {
             ...
             running = false;

+            if (synchronous) {
+                return; // no worker thread exists to signal/join
+            }
+
             // push an entry to signal the worker thread to stop
             ...
```

(the `...` markers stand for unchanged surrounding code; see
`common/log.cpp`'s `add()`/`resume()`/`pause()` methods for exact
context.)

### Run

```sh
./andesim run --mode fast --runtime linux --mem-size 2G \
  build-rv64-static/bin/llama-completion \
  -- -m models/Bonsai-4B-Q1_0.gguf -no-cnv \
     --prompt "The capital of France is" -n 32 -c 512 -t 1
```

**`--mem-size 2G`, not the `1G` `--runtime linux` default** (section
5.13): the model's own file-backed mmap (572 MB) plus its KV cache buffer
(576 MB at `-c 512`) together exceed 1G -- found by direct measurement,
not assumed; the roadmap's original "1G covers it" estimate did not
account for the KV cache. `-c 512` (context size) keeps that KV cache
buffer small and predictable; the default (`-c 0`, "loaded from model")
would size it off Bonsai-4B's trained context (32768), needing much more
memory for no benefit on a short prompt.

Text streams out through vlinux's UART as it generates. Measured on one
host: model load ~93s, prompt eval + a handful of tokens several more
minutes -- QEMU's functional (not accelerated) emulation of real RVV
matrix math on a real 4B-parameter model is slow by design (fast mode
trades speed for cycle-independent correctness, not throughput); `-n 32`
scales roughly linearly with token count. `exit()`'s known cleanup-handler
crash (section 5.13) means the process may show that exact crash
signature instead of a clean exit code -- the generated text itself is
unaffected either way (it is printed well before `exit()` runs).

`tests/vlinux/test_vlinux_llama_smoke.sh` automates this whole recipe
(manual-tier -- not part of `scripts/run_e2e.sh`; needs `LINUX_TOOLCHAIN`,
a pre-built `llama-completion`, and the GGUF model, all `config.env`-driven
via `LLAMA_CPP_DIR`/`LLAMA_COMPLETION_BIN`/`BONSAI_GGUF`).

### Measuring one kernel iteration under hybrid mode

The fast leg above proves correctness; `--mode hybrid` measures real
cycles for one kernel iteration -- a full token would take hours at
vsim's cycle-accurate clock (~9 kHz) even for a model this size, which is
why hybrid mode exists: everything up to the marker runs on the fast
QEMU leg (negligible time), and only the marked region runs on the
cycle-accurate leg.

```sh
./andesim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker --runtime linux --mem-size 2000M \
  --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium --step-timeout-ms 900000 \
  build-rv64-static/bin/llama-completion \
  -- -m models/Bonsai-4B-Q1_0.gguf -no-cnv \
     --prompt "The capital of France is" -n 32 -c 512 -t 1
```

(`--mem-size` stays under 2G -- the AE350 DRAM window's actual usable
ceiling is 2 GiB minus the V-scratch reservation, so `2000M` not `2G`.
`--step-timeout-ms` raises the per-leg spawn timeout past its 90s
default -- a real matmul on the cycle-accurate leg needs minutes; see the
`--step-timeout-ms` line in section 5.)

This needs `<andesim/ROI.h>` markers inside llama.cpp, bounding ONE
matvec-class operation (not a whole token) -- see `docs/llama_roi_howto.md`
for the exact patch and how the insertion point was chosen. Since ggml
builds its whole computation graph before executing it in one shot,
individual operations are not distinguishable at the application call-site
level (e.g. the per-layer forward-pass code) -- the markers go in ggml's
own generic per-node dispatch loop instead, gated by a counter so only
one specific node's execution is actually measured.

Confirmed end to end (2026-07-16): the command above, targeting layer 7's
V-projection matmul (the smallest matmul shape in this architecture --
see `docs/llama_roi_howto.md` for how the target node was chosen and why
the first, larger pick timed out even at 15 minutes), measured
**6528953 cycles**, identical across two independent runs (this hart is
deterministic single-hart, so a stable repeat cycle count is the
correctness signal, the same contract the small matvec fixture above it
already demonstrates). QEMU's hybrid leg enforces the real engine's
ELEN=32 vector-unit limit (unlike fast mode's own unconstrained CPU
string) -- getting a real llama.cpp build through it needed four small,
targeted upstream fixes to `ggml/src/ggml-cpu/{vec.cpp,arch/riscv/quants.c}`,
documented in `docs/llama_roi_howto.md` together with the exact
`ANDESIM_ROI_MUL_MAT_K` value and the reasoning for picking it.
